WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:01.900
SPEAKER: Hi.

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.670
I'm Nikhil, and
I'm going to talk

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.030
about Kubemci, a new
command line tool

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:10.870
to manage multicluster
ingresses.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.710
I'm going to talk about what
it does, why is it useful,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.360
and how it works.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.640
But before I get
started, I do want

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.560
to point out that Kubemci is not
an official supported product.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.270
It's an exploration.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:30.360
We want people to try it
out and see if it's useful.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.050
Secondly, as I
talk about Kubemci,

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.800
I'm going to assume knowledge
about Kubernetes and Kubernetes

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.220
Ingress.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.010
If you've used Kubernetes
Ingress with Google Cloud Load

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.790
Balancer to create
L7 load balancers,

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.280
you can now use
Kubemci to do the same

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.340
for multiple
Kubernetes clusters.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.350
Doesn't that sound awesome?

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.640
Let's see how it works.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.090
Our top use cases for Kubemci
are low latency and high

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.330
availability.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.519
Let's look at this
diagram on the right.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.760
We have two clusters, cluster
1 in GCP zone US Central 1-A

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.630
and cluster 2 in GCP zone
Europe West [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.880
Service [INAUDIBLE] exists
in both these clusters.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.860
Now, if user Alice
from California

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.750
wants to need
service [INAUDIBLE],,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.290
we want Google Cloud Load
Balancer to send that request

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:28.970
to cluster 1.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.540
Similarly, if user Bob from
Berlin sends the same request,

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.260
we want that request
to go to cluster 2.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.860
This ensures low latency.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.730
If cluster 2 goes
down, we still want

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.150
user Bob to be able to
read service [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.740
In that case, Google
Cloud Load Balancer

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.600
should send that
request to cluster 1.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.300
That ensures high availability.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.110
Now, how Kubemci works is it
has a Kubemci Create command,

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.310
where you can give it a
name, your Kubernetes Ingress

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.160
definition as ingress.yaml
and a list of clusters

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.170
in clusters.yaml.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:12.280
If you run this command,
Kubemci creates the ingress

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.220
in all the clusters, in
this case in both cluster

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:19.610
1 and cluster 2, and then
configures Google Cloud Load

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.860
Balancer to send the
traffic appropriately.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.590
To parse the list
of clusters, you

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.010
can give your
existing kubeconfig.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.832
Or in future, we want to
integrate with Kubernetes

00:02:31.832 --> 00:02:32.540
Cluster Registry.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.870
While this is a
high-level overview,

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.590
you can also take
a look at the code.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.220
It's open sourced.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.550
You can go to GitHub, and
you'll see all the code there.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.145
You can try it out and start
contributing to the code

00:02:51.145 --> 00:02:52.600
as well.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.720
But let's take a look at a demo.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.250
For this demo, I have
two clusters, one in US

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.010
and one in Europe.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.290
I have my zpkubeconfig
file, which has information

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:07.940
about both these clusters.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.710
So this is the
kubeconfig file that I'm

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.340
going to use with Kubemci.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.590
I'm using a zone printer
app for this example.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.940
What the app does is
it prints the GCP zone

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.650
that it is running in.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.940
I've created my zone
printer app in US cluster.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.230
And now I'm going to create the
same app in my Europe cluster

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.660
so that the same
app exists in both.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:34.990
Once we have this
setup ready, I am now

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:39.560
ready to use Kubemci to
create my load balancer.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.600
Let's look at
Kubemci Help first.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.470
Kubemci Help prints all
the available commands.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:53.030
There's Create,
there's Delete, there's

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.510
getstatus to get the status
of an existing ingress,

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.490
and Help and the
version of the tool.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.400
So let's look deeper
at Kubemci Create.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.570
We'll look at the Help
for Kubemci Create.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.250
This tells me I need
to pass a load balancer

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.950
name and some flags.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.260
GCP project is a required flag.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:20.420
The ingress spec
is a required flag.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:22.340
And kubeconfig is required.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.250
So let's try it out.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.780
Let's run Kubemci Create.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.650
I'm going to name my load
balancer zoneprinter-lb.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:34.700
I have an ingress spec,
and I use zpkubeconfig.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:41.210
It prints that it created the
ingress in both those clusters,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.800
then created health check,
backend service, URL map,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.020
target proxy, forwarding nodes.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.800
It's creating all the
resources required to configure

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.250
Google Cloud Load Balancer.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.830
Once it's done, I can
run getstatus command

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.160
to see the status
of my load balancer.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.790
To get the status, I
pass the name and the GCP

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.160
project it is in.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.190
And it tells me, yes, such
a load balancer exists,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.330
and this is the IP address.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.480
The load balancer is spread
across two clusters, cluster US

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:22.420
West and cluster EU
West, as I want it.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.640
GCLB, Google Cloud
Load Balancer,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.190
takes some time to configure.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:34.820
Once it's done, we can hit
this IP address to ensure

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.070
that it's working as expected.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.990
To do that, let's hit
that IP address from a VM

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.010
in my US cluster.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.210
As expected, it returns
that the request

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:58.540
was served from US West 1-A.
Let's do the same for Europe

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.528
cluster.

00:06:01.528 --> 00:06:04.310
And in this case,
we expect it to be

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.820
served from the Europe zone.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.010
It was served from Europe zone.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.240
So this shows that our setup
is working as expected.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.170
This is great.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.270
You should try it out.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.090
And we'd love to
get your feedback.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.750
Positive or not, any
feedback is appreciated.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:34.430
Thank you.

